WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.968
A decision has just been reached

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.970
in Mark Meadows bid to make his case

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:05.605
in the sprawling Georgia

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.140
election interference probe

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.209
moved to a federal court.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.711
A federal judge has just rejected

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:12.512
the former Trump

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:12.846
White House

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.881
chief of staff's arguments reach.

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.984
Dealing a major setback not only Meadows

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.419
but also potentially

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.487
the former president,

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.522
Donald Trump, who just yesterday

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.657
notified the judge overseeing the case

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:26.826
that he, too, may try to move his case

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.895
from state to federal court.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:29.562
CNN's Sara

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:30.397
Murray is following

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:31.431
all of these important

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:32.399
developments for us.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:33.299
What more can you tell us

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:34.701
about this monumental decision?

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:35.101
Well, Wolf,

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.269
we have been waiting for this

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:36.803
ruling, right?

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.104
Because Mark Meadows, the former

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.239
White House chief of staff,

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.074
was the first one out of the gate

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.976
saying he wants to move the charges

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.644
against him in state court

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:45.612
to federal court.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:46.713
A big part of the reason

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:47.514
that he was doing

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.149
this was because he wanted to go

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:49.916
to federal court

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:50.450
and argue

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.684
essentially that he had immunity

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.952
from these charges

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.387
and that they should be tossed out.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:55.221
And what we are now

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.023
hearing from the federal judge

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:58.058
is that Mark

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.993
Meadows did not meet the threshold

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.628
he needed to meet in order to move this

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:02.395
to federal court.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:02.829
In fact,

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.831
the judge says the court concludes

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:05.398
that Meadows

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:06.132
has not met

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.169
even the, quote, quite low threshold

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.203
for removal.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.504
Now, the judge does note

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:12.672
this is a novel case.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.641
This is a racketeering case.

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:16.142
But he goes on to say that

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:16.943
the court finds

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.378
that the color of the office

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.612
of White House Chief of staff,

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:21.281
which is what Mark Meadows need to prove,

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:22.048
needed to prove

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:23.316
that he was acting under

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.818
the color of the office,

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:26.920
did not include working with

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.289
or working for the Trump campaign

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:30.824
so essentially, these activities

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.459
that Meadows engaged in

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.561
after the 2020 election allegedly

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.563
related to trying to overturn Georgia's

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:37.597
election results

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.232
had more to do with campaign work

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:40.767
that had to do with his White House work

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.402
so they don't meet the threshold.

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:42.936
The other thing

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.070
I think is important to note

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.172
is that the judge goes on to say,

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.508
even though he is making this decision

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:49.476
about the Meadows

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.043
case, it's

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.444
not going to have any effect

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.279
on the outcome of other defendants.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.948
He's essentially going to let them

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:57.317
go forward, put their arguments forward.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:57.617
We know

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:58.351
Jeffrey Clarke,

00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.252
who's a former Justice

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.286
Department official, has

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.821
a hearing scheduled to do this.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:02.856
We know a couple of people

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:04.157
who served as fake electors

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.859
for Donald Trump in Georgia

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.193
also have a hearing scheduled

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.061
because they want to move

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:08.761
to federal court.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.896
So the judge is saying,

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:11.431
I will hear all your arguments

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:13.700
separately and I will decide them.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.502
But it certainly doesn't portend

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:16.369
well for them

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:16.803
that Mark

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.271
Meadows did not meet the bar here.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.205
You're absolutely right.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:19.839
Stay with us.

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:21.174
I want to bring in our legal

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:22.075
and political experts

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.811
to discuss this important development

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:25.411
right now.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:26.412
And Jamie Gangel,

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.014
how significant is this?

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:29.582
I think it's very significant,

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:31.618
even though, as Sara points out,

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:32.986
this ruling,

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.121
the judge said, does not

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.322
necessarily mean

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:38.525
it will carry over to other people.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.894
It does set a bar.

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:44.164
So I think it is bad

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.533
news in that the judge is saying,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.067
you know,

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.303
you have exceeded your role

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:51.938
as chief of staff.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:55.074
So that kind of argument

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.945
is going to to stand for other people.

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:01.381
The judge also mentioned the Hatch Act,

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:02.715
which limits

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.618
what an employee can do

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.221
that is political versus federal.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:12.258
So I think that this is going to

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.093
be bad news for other people

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.129
who want to go in this direction.

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.264
One thing I think we should remember.

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:21.534
Donald Trump wants to delay these cases.

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:24.571
His goal is to push all these

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:27.540
trials past the election.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:30.543
So even though this ruling has come down,

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.081
I don't think it means he won't try to

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.683
to do the same thing.

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.718
Let me get Gloria's reaction.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:41.221
A lengthy, 49 page decision

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.288
that was just released. Right.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.189
And we're just going through it.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.258
But I think my colleagues here

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.659
have gotten to the crux of the matter,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.662
which is that what the court is saying

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.765
is that Mark Meadows kind of overstepped

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:55.168
and that he wasn't acting

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:56.502
as a chief of staff should act

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.605
he was actually acting as an adjunct

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:00.273
to the Trump campaign.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.008
And when you mention the Hatch Act,

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.010
Wolf, you're not supposed to do that.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:04.310
You're at

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.979
when you're working in the White House,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.347
you work for the government.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.915
You don't work for a campaign.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.483
And there's a line here

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.151
that says the Constitution

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.287
does not provide any basis

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.723
for executive branch involved.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:17.257
With state

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.925
election and post-election

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.693
procedures, period.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:22.228
It is not your job

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:23.763
that was not part of your job.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.365
So you can't claim

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:26.499
that this should be moved

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.601
because you were just doing your job

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:31.037
because that's not your job

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:32.438
and you are acting

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.774
unofficially.

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:35.074
Yeah.

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:37.176
Just to add one quick sentence here,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.878
it says When questioned

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:41.047
about the scope of his authority,

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:41.648
Meadows

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.416
was unable to explain

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:45.418
the limits of his authority

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.220
other than his inability

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.655
to stump for the president

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:50.890
or work on behalf of the campaign.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.159
So so once again,

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:56.863
it shows there there is a clear line.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.198
But he didn't want to define it.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:01.100
And I think to Gloria's point to about,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:01.367
you know,

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.169
the difference between, again, state

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:04.637
and federal responsibilities

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.872
and running their elections

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:08.474
the judge gets to that in this order.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:09.075
We saw,

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:09.375
you know,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:10.777
some of this play out in the Meadows

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:11.611
hearing where Georgia

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:12.645
Secretary of State Brad

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.647
Raffensperger was a witness

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:15.548
and essentially said

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:16.382
the federal government

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:17.250
doesn't play a role

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.285
in certifying our elections

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:20.253
in this order.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:21.788
The judge says the court concludes

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:22.622
if it were to agree

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.657
with Meadows arguments regarding removal

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:25.224
and removal,

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:26.693
removal of federal court,

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:28.828
the court would have to turn a blind eye

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.797
to the express constitutional power

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.432
granted to the states

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.367
to determine their election procedures.

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.169
So he's again saying

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.370
this was a state job

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.905
to decide how they were going to certify

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:39.405
their elections,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.007
to decide to certify their elections.

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:41.541
This wasn't

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:43.376
something that fell under the role

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:44.877
of the White House chief of staff.

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:45.878
I want to bring in Michael Moore,

00:05:45.878 --> 00:05:46.512
a former U.S.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:47.580
attorney who's

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.848
also carefully taking a look

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:49.849
at this decision.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:50.983
The ruling says,

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:51.751
as you know, Michael,

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.786
that Meadows himself testified

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.822
that working for the Trump campaign

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:57.523
would be outside the scope

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:59.258
of a White House chief of staff.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:00.760
So what do you make of this decision?

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.563
Well, I'm glad to be with all of you.

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:05.064
It's not necessarily

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:06.199
an unexpected decision.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:07.467
We thought it could go either way.

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:09.736
I mean, and just getting the order.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:10.937
So it strikes me

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.805
that there will be an appeal directly

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:13.906
from this.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.208
It's interesting to hear

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:15.441
some of

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:16.309
the recitations

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:17.744
as it relates to the federal government's

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:19.245
role in state elections.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:21.481
There are about 12 federal agencies

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:22.982
that participate

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.851
in monitoring state elections.

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:25.518
And you can kind of

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:27.387
go down the list, DOJ,

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:28.454
make sure that voting rights

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:29.021
are protected.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:30.690
That's something that is in the purview

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:31.924
of the federal government.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.160
DHS, Homeland Security

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.561
monitor cyber crime

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:36.729
as it relates to elections,

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.431
even those that are held in the state

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:39.632
when they have all federal office.

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:41.801
So there will be arguments to be made

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.336
and there might be some

00:06:43.336 --> 00:06:44.670
room at the 11th Circuit

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.905
and certainly at the Supreme Court.

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.074
But we talk about what role it is.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:49.609
I think the question is,

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:50.810
was there a federal involvement

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:51.811
in certifying it?

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.913
Certainly there there is not

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:54.947
but an inquiry into

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:56.349
the election, I think, may

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:58.351
be an issue that comes

00:06:58.351 --> 00:06:59.886
up on the politics, but we'll hear that.

00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:00.820
I don't think this hurts

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.388
Trump necessarily.

00:07:02.388 --> 00:07:03.656
I think there'll be some other people

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:05.525
who will not find

00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:07.760
necessarily a favorable ear in the court.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.528
But certainly this

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:10.163
as it relates to Trump,

00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:11.798
ends up delaying things.

00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:13.433
And I think Jamie is correct

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:15.301
This case now goes to the 11th Circuit.

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:16.469
This appeal will it will

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:18.204
then wander its way to the Supreme Court.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:18.604
Have you heard

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.206
even the trial judge

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:21.507
earlier this week

00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:22.442
as he was suggesting

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:24.143
that those appeals are causing him

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:25.812
concern as he thinks about scheduled

00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:27.980
the case of postponing those trials?

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.615
Emily Copeland is with us as well,

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:31.150
a former federal prosecutor.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:31.918
Amy Lee,

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.786
what do you think this means for Trump?

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:35.521
His lawyers, as you well know,

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.222
have signaled

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:37.924
that he will try to move his case

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:39.859
from a state court to a federal court

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:42.462
Hey, Wolf.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.162
Well, his lawyers

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:44.730
actually said we're thinking about it.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.032
They didn't make a commitment.

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:47.266
And they have 30 days

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:48.568
from the date of his arraignment.

00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:49.569
And I would say here

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:50.703
from when he filed the waiver

00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:51.571
of formal arraignment

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.372
to make that decision.

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:54.240
I think all of us

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:55.608
think that Mark Meadows,

00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:57.810
if anyone had the best chance

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.946
of getting the case removed

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:00.913
because he did have

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:01.681
some duties

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:03.483
that he said were inarguably

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.884
for the president.

00:08:04.884 --> 00:08:05.585
You know,

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:06.552
it was interesting,

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:07.687
the breakdown in this order.

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:09.088
The court said,

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:10.323
look at the heart of the matter

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.090
when you consider

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.158
what the act is,

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:13.226
you can't just say that

00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:14.794
any old act is enough.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:15.728
You can't say that

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:16.863
scheduling calls

00:08:16.863 --> 00:08:18.865
or setting meetings were over

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:19.532
overt act here.

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:21.434
It had to go to the heart of the matter

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:21.934
I think Mr.

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.135
Trump's problem is going to be

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:24.403
is that all of these calls

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:25.705
went to the heart of the matter.

00:08:25.705 --> 00:08:27.540
He was acting as candidate Trump

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.108
rather than President Trump.

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:30.076
And I'm sure his lawyers

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:30.977
are going to give this order

00:08:30.977 --> 00:08:32.011
a close read.

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:33.279
Let me go back to Sara understanding

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:33.913
you're getting some

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:34.780
more information

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.650
on how this order was unfolding

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:38.751
and what happens next.

00:08:38.751 --> 00:08:39.185
Yeah, I mean,

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:40.586
I want to talk a little bit about again,

00:08:40.586 --> 00:08:41.854
we talked about the Hatch Act

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:42.722
and whether or not

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:43.256
that was going to be

00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:45.558
a persuasive argument for prosecutors

00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:46.859
who said this sort of defines

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.060
that if you're a federal official,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.362
you can't be engaged

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:50.296
in getting paid

00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:51.364
by the federal government.

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:52.765
You can't get involved in power.

00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:53.599
Right. Exactly.

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:55.468
And the judge writes to this in his order

00:08:55.468 --> 00:08:56.969
and says The Hatch Act is helpful

00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:58.004
in defining the outer

00:08:58.004 --> 00:08:59.572
limit of the scope of the White House.

00:08:59.572 --> 00:09:00.773
Chief of staff's authority.

00:09:00.773 --> 00:09:02.842
The state argues and Meadows agrees

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:05.177
that he is bound by the Hatch Act,

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.746
a law that prohibits federal employees

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:08.681
from engaging in political activity.

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:10.683
These prohibitions on executive

00:09:10.683 --> 00:09:11.551
branch employees,

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:12.485
including the White House

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:13.486
chief of staff,

00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:15.288
reinforce the court's conclusion

00:09:15.288 --> 00:09:16.822
that Meadows has not shown

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:18.291
how his actions relate

00:09:18.291 --> 00:09:19.458
to the scope of his federal

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:20.660
executive branch office.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.128
Federal officer removal

00:09:22.128 --> 00:09:24.363
is thereby in opposite.

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.832
Again, this is an appealable decision.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:27.333
And I think

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:28.768
just because this is something

00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:29.802
that Mark Meadows loss

00:09:29.802 --> 00:09:31.270
does not mean that this is the route

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.505
that Donald Trump's attorneys

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:33.506
are not going to go down.

00:09:33.506 --> 00:09:35.608
I think that the reason that they said

00:09:35.608 --> 00:09:37.276
they may remove to federal court

00:09:37.276 --> 00:09:38.678
is that they wanted to see how

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:39.145
this was going

00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:40.413
to play out for Mark Meadows.

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:42.515
They wanted to see what arguments

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:44.617
maybe seemed persuasive to the judge

00:09:44.617 --> 00:09:46.185
and what arguments fell flat,

00:09:46.185 --> 00:09:48.120
because this will be the same judge

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:48.988
who is ultimately going

00:09:48.988 --> 00:09:50.856
to hear the arguments from Donald Trump

00:09:50.856 --> 00:09:52.658
if he tries to move this case

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:53.526
to federal court.

00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:55.027
And to Michael Moore's point.

00:09:55.027 --> 00:09:56.262
This was something that came up

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:56.762
in this sort

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:58.531
of scheduling back and forth about

00:09:58.531 --> 00:09:59.699
are we going to go forward

00:09:59.699 --> 00:10:01.434
with all of these 19 defendants

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:02.668
going to trial

00:10:02.668 --> 00:10:04.003
in October of this year,

00:10:04.003 --> 00:10:05.171
or is it just going to be Ken

00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:06.639
Hasbro and Sidney Powell?

00:10:06.639 --> 00:10:07.306
And so

00:10:07.306 --> 00:10:09.241
it'll be more for that state court

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:10.776
judge to chew over as he tries

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:12.411
to set out a schedule for these folks.

